# Copy to .env and fill in your values.

# ── Deployment posture ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The single most important setting. Whether anyone but you can reach this
# server cannot be detected at runtime — behind a reverse proxy every caller
# looks local — so it is declared here, and these protections follow from it:
#
#                          local (default)      hosted
#   server-side API keys   lent to localhost    never; users bring their own
#   rate limits            none                 per caller; see Overrides below
#   LLM call timeout       600s                 90s
#   rethon computations    unbounded            20 elements, 60s
#
# The server writes nothing to disk in either mode: the browser keeps the
# working state, and Markdown export is the way out of it.
#
# "local" means uvicorn and the browser on the same machine. Anything else —
# a LAN, a tunnel, a VPS, a container behind nginx — is "hosted".
DEPLOYMENT=local

# Per-provider API keys, keyed by base URL. The backend selects the matching
# key automatically when the frontend switches providers.
# For local Ollama use: LLM_API_KEYS={"http://localhost:11434/v1":"ollama"}
LLM_API_KEYS={"https://api.openai.com/v1":"sk-...","https://api.anthropic.com/v1":"sk-ant-...","https://api.mistral.ai/v1":"..."}

# Default model used when the frontend does not send an x-model header.
# For local Ollama use the name from `ollama create <name>`, e.g. ministral
DEFAULT_MODEL=gpt-4o-mini

# Output cap for a single Anthropic completion (the Messages API requires one).
# Not sent to OpenAI-compatible providers. Raise it if relation or argument
# detection over a large element set returns truncated, unparseable JSON — the
# backend logs a warning when a reply stops at the cap.
# LLM_MAX_TOKENS=4096

# Seconds one provider call may run before it is abandoned. Follows DEPLOYMENT
# (local: 600, the SDK default; hosted: 90). Raise it locally only if a slow model
# times out on large element sets. Connecting is always capped at 10s.
# LLM_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=
# Retries on connection errors, 429 and 5xx (the SDKs' own default is 2).
# LLM_MAX_RETRIES=1

# Comma-separated list of allowed CORS origins. No wildcards permitted.
# Dev: http://localhost:5173   Prod: https://your-app.com
# Empty when one proxy serves the page and routes /api here: every request is
# then same-origin, and there is no one to allow.
CORS_ORIGINS=http://localhost:5173


# ── Access control ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Comma-separated. When non-empty, every /api route except /api/health requires
# one of these values in an x-app-token header. Empty by default — a local
# install has nobody to authenticate.
#
# FOR API CLIENTS, NOT THE WEB APP. The site never sends x-app-token, so setting
# this on an instance the site talks to locks the site out of every route but
# /api/health. It fails closed — nothing leaks — but nothing works either. Leave
# it empty behind the web app, as the published deployment does; set it only on
# an instance reached by scripts or other clients that send the header.
#
# For such clients, issue ONE TOKEN PER PARTICIPANT for a class or study: the rate limiter buckets
# by whichever token matched, so distinct tokens give each person their own
# allowance. A single shared token authenticates fine but puts everyone in one
# bucket, which for a seminar room on shared wifi is the same problem as keying
# on IP.
# APP_ACCESS_TOKENS=alice-8f3a,bob-2c91,carol-77de


# ── Overrides ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Each of these follows DEPLOYMENT unless set. Only set one to depart from the
# posture above.

# Whether a caller that sends no key of its own may spend a server-side one.
# Follows DEPLOYMENT (local: yes, hosted: no). Set true on a hosted instance only
# if you run uvicorn behind --forwarded-allow-ips=<proxy ip>, so that
# request.client is the real caller rather than your proxy — and NEVER with
# --forwarded-allow-ips="*", under which "x-forwarded-for: 127.0.0.1" from any
# caller reads as local.
# ALLOW_LOOPBACK_SERVER_KEYS=

# Four independent per-caller caps, requests per minute; 0 disables that one.
# All four are counted in one worker's memory, so run a single uvicorn worker.
# Each follows DEPLOYMENT unless set (local: 0, hosted: as noted).
#
# Without APP_ACCESS_TOKENS a caller is its peer address. Behind a reverse proxy,
# set TRUSTED_PROXY_HOPS below, or every visitor is the proxy and shares one
# allowance. The backend logs a warning when it sees this.
#
# LLM calls — one outbound request each, mostly spent waiting. Hosted: 60.
# LLM_RATE_LIMIT_PER_MINUTE=
#
# /simulate, /score_per_round — each runs a whole process to a fixed point in one
# request and holds the interpreter while it does. Hosted: 5. A handful a minute
# is already generous; /score_per_round runs one simulation per round.
# SIMULATION_RATE_LIMIT_PER_MINUTE=
#
# /step — costs what a simulation costs, but is pressed once per step by someone
# walking an evolution forward, and an evolution takes as many steps as it takes.
# Hosted: 30. On the simulation allowance it refused its sixth press.
# STEPPING_RATE_LIMIT_PER_MINUTE=
#
# /quick_score, /score_changes — analytic, and NOT user-initiated: the frontend
# fires quick_score on every edit AND once per suggestion card, so a tab of nine
# suggestions makes ten calls and accepting one re-fires all ten. No debounce
# anywhere on that path. Hosted: 300. Set this high or leave it alone — a cap
# that bites here is invisible to the person it is biting, because a failed score
# renders as a blank badge, and looks like a broken app rather than a busy one.
# SCORING_RATE_LIMIT_PER_MINUTE=

# Number of proxies in front of uvicorn that append to x-forwarded-for. The
# rate limiter then identifies a caller by the entry that many places from the
# right — the one the outermost proxy wrote — and never by anything to the left
# of it, which the caller chose. 0 (the default) uses the socket peer.
# Cloud Run, Fly, Render: 1. Behind a load balancer that also appends: 2.
# Never on a port reachable directly: the caller writes the rightmost entry then.
# TRUSTED_PROXY_HOPS=0

# Most elements taking part in arguments that any rethon computation will accept;
# 0 disables the cap. Follows DEPLOYMENT (local: 0, hosted: 24).
#
# This bounds the cost of ONE request, which a rate limit cannot. The work grows
# like the Fibonacci numbers in the elements arguments tie together, about x1.6
# per element: on a development machine a dense chain at depth 2 takes ~2s at 20,
# ~4s at 22, ~15s at 24 and ~50s at 28 — some ten times less than before the
# simulation's theory was restricted to principles (see config.py). An element no argument mentions adds almost
# nothing, which is why this counts only argued ones. A slow request is not slow
# for its caller alone: computations run one at a time per worker pool, so every
# other visitor's waits behind it, for up to SIMULATION_TIMEOUT_SECONDS.
# MAX_ARGUED_ELEMENTS=

# Largest sentence pool (element count in all) any rethon computation will
# accept; 0 disables the cap. Follows DEPLOYMENT (local: 0, hosted: 50). Looser
# than the cap above: an unargued element is cheap but not free — History scores
# every step of the process, one simulation each.
# MAX_SIMULATION_ELEMENTS=

# Deepest neighbourhood a simulation's local search may use; 0 leaves the
# request's own limit of 4. Follows DEPLOYMENT (local: 0, hosted: 2). Depth 3
# costs about ten times depth 2. The Simulate tab reads this from /api/health
# and, where it is set, always searches at it rather than offering a choice.
# MAX_NEIGHBOURHOOD_DEPTH=

# Seconds one rethon computation may run before its worker process is killed and
# the caller gets a 504; 0 disables it. Counts computing, not waiting in line.
# Follows DEPLOYMENT (local: 0, hosted: 60).
# SIMULATION_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=

# Bytes a request body may carry before it is refused with 413, unread; 0
# disables it. Follows DEPLOYMENT (local: 0, hosted: 10485760, i.e. 10 MiB —
# the size of the compose stack's nginx client_max_body_size, kept in step).
# MAX_REQUEST_BODY_BYTES=

# Worker processes for rethon computations: one pool for full simulations, one for
# the score lookups the frontend fires on every edit, so the score badges never
# wait behind a simulation. Each worker is a core and a copy of rethon in memory.
# SIMULATION_WORKERS=1
# SCORING_WORKERS=1
